Subject: Build Closures From A Cached Shape Instead Of Defining Them

What this does
  `js_closure()` builds a bytecode function object from a per-realm shape
  template in one JS_NewObjectFromShape() call, writing `length`, `name` and
  `prototype` straight into the property array, instead of creating a bare
  object and adding those three properties one shape transition at a time.

Why we need it
  Every closure of a given (func_kind, has_prototype) ends at the identical
  final shape -- {length, name} or {length, name, prototype} -- so the two or
  three JS_DefinePropertyValue() calls were shape transitions that rebuilt the
  same shape for every function object a program creates: a shape-hash lookup, a
  refcount swap, an atom dup and, for the third property, a js_realloc of the
  property array from 2 to 4 slots.

What it adds
  `JSContext.closure_shape[8]`, indexed by func_kind * 2 + has_prototype,
  filled lazily from the shape the transition path actually produced. Being the
  canonical HASHED shape is load-bearing: js_shape_prepare_update() only clones
  a shape it can see is shared, so redefining, adding to or deleting from one
  closure must not mutate the template every later closure adopts. The cache is
  marked and freed with the context, and the prototype is re-checked on every
  hit, so a context whose class prototypes move cannot adopt a stale shape. The
  generator arm still creates its prototype object per closure, and the autoinit
  word is reproduced exactly as JS_DefineAutoInitProperty() encodes it.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No

